WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.569
WIL WILLIS:
Bladesmiths, now it's

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.836
time to send you back
to your home forges

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:05.538
to recreate this iconic weapon--

00:00:09.909 --> 00:00:13.279
J. Neilson's war scythe.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.147
DOUG MARCAIDA: Next up,
I'll be pre-testing--

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:18.284
[laughs maniacally] --J.
Neilson's war scythe.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:21.621
WIL WILLIS: This war scythe was
personally created by our very

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:24.290
own Beast from the East.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.526
This war scythe is
a menacing variation

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:29.963
on a centuries-old peasants'
weapon from Middle Age Europe.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:31.531
Ready for battle at
a moment's notice,

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:34.233
farmers would take their
scythes from work to war

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.236
during uprisings, riots,
or calls to battle.

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:40.205
This weapon uses a long shaft
to generate massive power

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.142
to cut down enemies, while
its lethal sharp point

00:00:43.208 --> 00:00:45.511
could be thrust into
infantry and cavalry.

00:00:45.578 --> 00:00:48.314
This multifaceted
scythe features a fork

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.150
opposite the blade as a
brutal piercing element

00:00:51.216 --> 00:00:53.152
and a small blade at
the bottom of the pole

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:54.820
to finish off an opponent.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.956
Scythes are represented
in pop culture

00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:59.625
as sinister and ominous
weapons symbolizing death

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:04.697
and is most notoriously
wielded by the grim reaper.

00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:06.665
DOUG MARCAIDA: It was a very
interesting weapon because you

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:08.867
got a scythe and you get a
blade-- a curved blade, here--

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:10.102
on the edges on the outside.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.604
Have I ever tested
anything like that?

00:01:11.671 --> 00:01:12.605
No.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:16.175
And when I did, pure heaven.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:20.880
WIL WILLIS: The kill test
master, Doug Marcaida.

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.782
[maniacal laughter]

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.617
Yeah, he just looks
like a murderer.

00:01:29.221 --> 00:01:30.489
DOUG MARCAIDA: So
this is a weapon,

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:32.859
if you're not careful or
are unfamiliar with it,

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:34.259
you can actually hurt yourself.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:35.895
When you've got
blades everywhere

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:38.031
facing the wrong way-- you've
got the front, the back here,

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:40.233
and the hook blade, and
everything else-- you really

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.134
have to be used to
handling weapons

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.203
and knowing where
everything dangerous,

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:48.340
everything sharp is at.

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.343
Every time you swing, you
make sure that you don't

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:51.711
swing it back to cut yourself.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:58.017
To test J. Neilson's war
scythe, because we didn't have

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:00.186
another weapon just
like it, we had

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:03.022
to make a mockup of it that
was similar to the dimensions

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:04.524
that J. presented.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:05.892
It was very interesting.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:08.628
It was very revealing on how to
fight with something like this

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:10.163
without cutting yourself.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.831
As you're coming forward,
there's a blade right there,

00:02:11.897 --> 00:02:13.199
so I got to be careful of that.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.001
As I'm going there, there's
another blade over here.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:16.769
Just wielding that
thing, it really

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:20.940
gave me a chance to actually
learn to adjust and create

00:02:21.006 --> 00:02:23.242
a movement that
keeps me safe, but at

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:26.479
the same time do maximum damage
to what's in front of me.

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:38.491
[dramatic music]

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.313
All right, Alex.

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:04.350
The balance is light, allowing
it to be a very fast weapon

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.885
that you can use little
hand, long-handed,

00:03:06.952 --> 00:03:08.287
or for close quarters.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.723
Your weapon will kill.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:14.193
All right, Trevor.

00:03:14.259 --> 00:03:15.594
Trick or treat?
- Treat.

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:16.729
Indeed.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.971
[rock music]

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:50.129
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Trevor.

00:03:50.195 --> 00:03:53.399
The tip alone penetrates
deeply into the gut.

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:55.401
And the edges here
are sharp enough to,

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.236
well, make it lose his head.

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:59.171
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:00.506
Thank you, sir.

00:04:05.277 --> 00:04:06.746
J. NEILSON: All right, smiths.

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:11.217
Welcome to the strength test,
the dreaded coffin challenge.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:13.852
I'm going to take these bones
and beat them repeatedly

00:04:13.952 --> 00:04:15.454
into the blade of your scythe.

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:18.090
And Alex, you're locked
in and ready to go first.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:19.025
You ready?

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:20.126
Yep.

00:04:20.226 --> 00:04:22.695
That's what I want to hear.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:26.366
[dramatic music]

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:46.819
Alex, you survived.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.187
Good job.

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:49.588
The only issue I had-- you
have some slight rolling

00:04:49.688 --> 00:04:50.956
on the edge.

00:04:51.023 --> 00:04:54.226
And the head of this moves,
so it's not secure anymore.

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:55.661
But your edge held up great.

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:56.662
Good job.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.063
Thank you.

00:04:58.163 --> 00:04:59.331
How are you feeling, Trevor.

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.767
- Pretty good, right now.
- Good?

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.402
We'll see how you
feel in a minute.

00:05:06.171 --> 00:05:09.776
[rock music]

00:05:21.553 --> 00:05:23.388
Nice job, Trevor-- held up well.

00:05:23.455 --> 00:05:25.792
Your edge is still
good, nice and sharp.

00:05:25.892 --> 00:05:27.560
Everything's still tight.

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:28.827
Good job.

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:29.862
Thank you.

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:35.268
All right, bladesmiths.

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:38.004
This is the sharpness
test, the zombie surprise.

00:05:41.073 --> 00:05:43.942
To test the edge of your weapon,
I'm going to take your scythe

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:47.880
and cut through this rope,
thereby releasing a surprise.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:48.948
(LAUGHING) All right.

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:49.982
Alex, you're up first.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:51.050
You ready?

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.320
Certainly.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.024
[rock music]

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:17.910
[bleep]

00:06:21.413 --> 00:06:22.348
DOUG MARCAIDA: All right, Alex.

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:24.450
It cut easily on the rope.

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:29.221
When you're puncturing this,
it cuts through and slices.

00:06:29.288 --> 00:06:30.689
It'll cut.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.324
- Thank you.
- All right, Trevor.

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:34.026
Your turn, sir.
You ready?

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:35.128
Hell yeah.

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:42.668
[thunder rolls]

00:06:45.003 --> 00:06:48.608
[rock music]

00:07:12.698 --> 00:07:13.866
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Trevor.

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:16.234
The edge that you
have here lends itself

00:07:16.301 --> 00:07:17.870
to cut the rope much easier.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.605
When you're
puncturing, it starts

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:21.273
to cut all the way through.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.875
Overall, sir, it will cut.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:23.976
Thank you.

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:28.614
WIL WILLIS: All
right, bladesmiths.

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:30.449
You guys have done fantastic
work, but only one of you

00:07:30.515 --> 00:07:32.284
can be the "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:33.719
And that champion is--

00:07:36.822 --> 00:07:37.757
Trevor.

00:07:37.824 --> 00:07:38.757
Congratulations.

00:07:38.857 --> 00:07:40.892
You are the new "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:07:40.993 --> 00:07:44.029
Alex, unfortunately, your
blade took some damage

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:45.063
in our strength test.

00:07:45.163 --> 00:07:46.665
You did great work, my friend.

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:50.536
But at this time, I have to ask
you to please exit the forge.

00:07:50.602 --> 00:07:52.671
ALEX: Ultimately, it came
down to the construction.

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:54.373
I think I definitely
made a blade

00:07:54.473 --> 00:07:56.008
that can intimidate anyone.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:07:59.978
WIL WILLIS: Well,
Trevor, the judges all

00:08:00.078 --> 00:08:02.414
agree you made a damn near
perfect war scythe, which

00:08:02.514 --> 00:08:04.150
has earned you the title
of "Forged in Fire"

00:08:04.217 --> 00:08:06.252
champion and a
check for $10,000.

00:08:06.318 --> 00:08:07.419
Good job, brother.

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:09.888
Come on forward and
shake our hands.

00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:11.591
TREVOR: I won!

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:12.992
Woo!

00:08:13.058 --> 00:08:15.127
I think the biggest thing I
learned was to keep on trying.

00:08:15.193 --> 00:08:18.130
If something doesn't go your
way, don't get upset about it.

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:20.266
Just keep moving forward.

00:08:20.366 --> 00:08:21.934
This was a perfect
Halloween experience.

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:23.535
I feel like I just
went trick-or-treating,

00:08:23.635 --> 00:08:25.471
and I got a pillowcase
full of candy.

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:27.539
[theme music]

00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:31.010
[spooky laughter, scream]

